# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Build a minimal three-node local metadata acting set with a controlled primary and a common durable log prefix.
- [x] Create the suspected race state: primary pending slot already replaced with same payload at a later log index, while a non-primary has a divergent intervening durable log entry.
- [x] Verify `reissue_pending_metadata_command` returns the same-payload replacement instead of failing closed with `MetadataCommandLogConflict`.
- [x] Verify replica acceptance/application allows the later command on nodes that lack the intervening log entry.
- [x] Verify durable postconditions show metadata applied with non-contiguous/divergent command-log state.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC plus LLDB trace. Direct crash attempt: `cargo test -p storage poc_reissue_same_payload_replacement_bypasses_divergent_non_primary_history -- --nocapture` completed successfully, which is expected for this logical integrity bug rather than a memory-safety crash. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable (`valgrind: command not found`); ASan was not usable because the installed Rust toolchain is stable and rejects `-Z` sanitizer flags. Debugger validation used LLDB with a breakpoint at `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:477`, and execution stopped exactly on `return Ok((current.payload() == command.payload()).then_some(current));` from `StorageCluster::reissue_pending_metadata_command`, called by the PoC test.

Evidence: the PoC constructs a three-node metadata PG with a common durable prefix at log index 1, inserts a primary pending replacement at log index 3 with the same payload as a stale command, and applies a divergent non-primary-only command at log index 2. The call at the PoC lines `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:4851-4855` returns the index-3 replacement instead of a `MetadataCommandLogConflict`. Applying that returned command succeeds at `local.rs:4860-4862`. The postconditions at `local.rs:4868-4882` prove corruption/non-contiguity: every node has max durable metadata command log index 3 and the second bucket metadata is present, but nodes 1 and 2 do not have the intervening occupant bucket and their contiguous `metadata_command_replica_state.applied_log_index` remains 1.

This matches the suspected vulnerable chain: `cluster.rs:473-477` returns a same-payload current primary pending slot before checking acting-set max history; `cluster.rs:484-497` has the same bypass even after detecting a higher acting-set index; `pg_store.rs:2051-2058` accepts a command when there is no entry at that exact log index; `pg_store.rs:5389-5393` applies metadata before recording/advancing; and the advancement loop around `pg_store.rs:2492-2538` breaks on a missing next contiguous log entry, allowing metadata from a later command to persist while the replica state remains behind.

